===============================================================================
INTERFACES IN JAVA
package OOPS.Abstraction.Interfaces
===============================================================================

Read OOPS/Abstraction/Notes and AbstractClass/Notes first.

You already have one interface file in the repo -- src/Interface/
ClassImplementsInterface.java. It proves you can declare one. It does not
show you why they exist. This package is the real version.


    CONTENTS
        PART 1  : The syntax, and the words the compiler adds for you
        PART 2  : implements -- the rules
        PART 3  : Multiple interfaces -- the reason interfaces exist
        PART 4  : Interfaces extending interfaces
        PART 5  : default methods (Java 8) -- and why they were added
        PART 6  : static and private methods in an interface (Java 8 / 9)
        PART 7  : The diamond problem, and the "class always wins" rule
        PART 8  : Interface vs abstract class -- the full table
        PART 9  : Functional interfaces, marker interfaces, sealed
        PART 10 : Where you will meet interfaces next -- Collections
        PART 11 : Traps and interview questions
        PART 12 : Practice list


===============================================================================
PART 1 : THE SYNTAX, AND THE WORDS THE COMPILER ADDS FOR YOU
===============================================================================

WHAT YOU WRITE                      WHAT THE COMPILER ACTUALLY SEES

    interface Drawable {                interface Drawable {
        int SIDES = 4;                      public static final int SIDES = 4;
        void draw();                        public abstract void draw();
    }                                   }

Those invisible modifiers explain almost every interface rule. Learn this
one table and most of the traps disappear:

    FIELDS      always  public static final     -> they are CONSTANTS
                        must be initialised where declared
                        cannot be changed, ever
                        there are NO instance fields in an interface

    METHODS     always  public
                        abstract, unless marked default or static

    NAMING      constants are written IN_CAPS, by convention

THE CONSEQUENCES

  * An interface has NO STATE. It cannot remember anything per object.
    This is the single biggest difference from an abstract class.

  * An interface has NO CONSTRUCTOR. Nothing to initialise, so nothing to
    construct. `new Drawable()` is a compile error.

  * You cannot write  private void helper();  as an abstract method.
    Abstract interface methods are public by definition. (Since Java 9 you
    CAN write a private method WITH a body -- see PART 6.)

  * An interface cannot have instance initializer blocks. Static blocks are
    also not allowed. (Constants are initialised inline instead.)


===============================================================================
PART 2 : implements -- THE RULES
===============================================================================

        class Circle implements Drawable {
            @Override
            public void draw() {  ... }       // note: public is MANDATORY
        }

  1. The implementing class must provide a body for EVERY abstract method,
     or be declared abstract itself. Same two choices as with an abstract
     class -- AbstractClass/Notes PART 4.

  2. The implementing method MUST be public. This is not a special rule:
     it is the ordinary overriding rule that you may never REDUCE
     visibility, and the interface method was already public. Leave out
     `public` and you get:

            attempting to assign weaker access privileges; was public

     This error catches everybody once. Now it will not catch you.

  3. extends comes BEFORE implements, and there can be only one extends:

            class Bird extends Animal implements Flyable, Swimmable { }

  4. You can use the interface as a REFERENCE TYPE, and you constantly will:

            Drawable d = new Circle();        // upcasting to an interface
            d.draw();                         // Circle's version runs

     You cannot instantiate the interface itself:

            Drawable d = new Drawable();      // ERROR

     Same distinction as an abstract class: the reference is fine, the
     object is not.

  5. An interface reference can only see methods DECLARED IN THE INTERFACE.
     Circle may have 20 methods; through a Drawable reference you can call
     exactly the ones Drawable declares. The reference type limits the
     view -- Polymorphism/Notes, mistake 4.


===============================================================================
PART 3 : MULTIPLE INTERFACES -- THE REASON INTERFACES EXIST
===============================================================================

This is the payoff. A class gets ONE superclass and as MANY interfaces as
it likes:

        class Duck extends Bird implements Swimmable, Walkable, Flyable { }

Java deliberately does not allow multiple class inheritance -- that is the
classic "diamond problem" of C++, where two parents provide conflicting
STATE and the compiler cannot decide which copy you get.

Interfaces sidestep it: since Java 8 they can carry behaviour, but they
still carry NO STATE, so there is never a conflicting copy of a field to
choose between. Java therefore gives you:

        multiple inheritance of TYPE and BEHAVIOUR    -> allowed
        multiple inheritance of STATE                 -> not allowed

WHAT THIS BUYS YOU -- grouping unrelated classes

        interface Serviceable { void service(); }

        class Car       extends Vehicle    implements Serviceable { }
        class Generator extends Machine    implements Serviceable { }
        class Lift      extends Equipment  implements Serviceable { }

        Serviceable[] jobs = { new Car(), new Generator(), new Lift() };
        for (Serviceable s : jobs) s.service();

Those three classes have NO common ancestor. No abstract class could ever
have put them in one array. The interface did it in one line.

That is the IS-A vs CAN-DO distinction from Abstraction/Notes PART 6, and
it is why the rule of thumb is "default to an interface".

SPENDING YOUR ONE extends

        Think of `extends` as a single, non-refundable slot. The moment you
        make Car extend Vehicle, Car can never extend anything else, for
        the life of the program. Interfaces cost you nothing, so they are
        the safe default.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : INTERFACES EXTENDING INTERFACES
===============================================================================

An interface uses `extends`, not `implements`, and may extend SEVERAL:

        interface Readable  { void read(); }
        interface Writable  { void write(); }

        interface ReadWrite extends Readable, Writable {
            void flush();
        }

        class File implements ReadWrite {
            public void read()  { }      // all three are now required
            public void write() { }
            public void flush() { }
        }

    interface extends interface        -> legal, and multiple
    interface extends class            -> ILLEGAL. An interface has no
                                          superclass at all, not even Object.
    class      implements interface    -> legal, and multiple
    class      extends class           -> legal, exactly one

Your own src/Interface/ClassImplementsInterface.java implements A1 and S2,
which both declare m1(). That is legal and one method satisfies both --
identical abstract signatures merge harmlessly. It only becomes a problem
when they are DEFAULT methods. See PART 7.


===============================================================================
PART 5 : default METHODS (JAVA 8)
===============================================================================

        interface Serviceable {
            void service();                          // abstract

            default void reminder() {                // has a BODY
                System.out.println("service due in 6 months");
            }
        }

An implementing class gets reminder() for free and may override it.

WHY THEY WERE ADDED -- this is the interesting part, and it is a good
interview answer.

    Before Java 8, adding a method to an interface broke every class that
    implemented it, everywhere in the world. The java.util.List interface
    is implemented by thousands of classes in millions of programs. Adding
    one method would have failed to compile all of them.

    Java 8 needed to add forEach() and stream() to Collection. default
    methods made that possible: existing classes inherited a working body
    and kept compiling. This is called BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY, and it is
    the reason the feature exists.

    So when you write  list.forEach(...)  later, you are calling a default
    method defined in java.lang.Iterable.

RULES
    - a default method may be overridden, but does not have to be
    - it may call other methods of the interface, including abstract ones
    - it CANNOT access instance fields, because there are none
    - it cannot be static and default at the same time

DO NOT ABUSE THEM. A default method is for evolving a published interface,
or for a genuinely universal convenience. It is not a replacement for an
abstract class -- it still cannot hold state.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : static AND private METHODS IN AN INTERFACE
===============================================================================

STATIC METHODS (Java 8)

        interface Validator {
            static boolean isBlank(String s) {
                return s == null || s.trim().isEmpty();
            }
        }

        Validator.isBlank("  ");        // call it on the INTERFACE

    THE TRAP -- interface static methods are NOT inherited.

        class Form implements Validator { }

        Form.isBlank("x");              // ERROR -- does not compile
        new Form().isBlank("x");        // ERROR too
        Validator.isBlank("x");         // the ONLY way

    This is different from an abstract class, where a static method IS
    inherited by the subclass name. Expect this as a trick question.

    Why it exists: it lets an interface carry its own helper methods
    instead of needing a separate "XyzUtils" class next to it.

PRIVATE METHODS (Java 9)

        interface Report {
            default void daily()   { print("daily"); }
            default void monthly() { print("monthly"); }

            private void print(String kind) {          // Java 9+
                System.out.println("Generating " + kind + " report");
            }
        }

    Purely to remove duplication between default methods. They are not
    inherited and not visible outside the interface -- which is the whole
    point, because before Java 9 any shared helper had to be public.

    private static methods are also allowed, to help static methods.

SUMMARY OF WHAT AN INTERFACE MAY CONTAIN, BY VERSION

        Java 7 and before   constants, abstract methods
        Java 8              + default methods, + static methods
        Java 9              + private methods, + private static methods
        Java 17             + sealed / permits

    You are on Java 21, so all of it is available.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : THE DIAMOND PROBLEM, AND "CLASS ALWAYS WINS"
===============================================================================

CASE 1 -- two interfaces, two identical ABSTRACT methods

        interface A1 { void m1(); }
        interface S2 { void m1(); }
        class C implements A1, S2 {
            public void m1() { }         // one body satisfies both. Fine.
        }

    No ambiguity. There is nothing to choose between -- neither has a body.
    This is exactly your src/Interface/ClassImplementsInterface.java.

CASE 2 -- two interfaces, two identical DEFAULT methods

        interface A { default void hello() { System.out.println("A"); } }
        interface B { default void hello() { System.out.println("B"); } }

        class C implements A, B { }          // COMPILE ERROR

            class C inherits unrelated defaults for hello() from types A and B

    Now there ARE two bodies and the compiler refuses to guess. YOU must
    resolve it, in one of two ways:

        class C implements A, B {
            @Override
            public void hello() {
                System.out.println("C's own version");   // write a new one
            }
        }

    or pick one explicitly, with the special super syntax:

        class C implements A, B {
            @Override
            public void hello() {
                A.super.hello();             // note: InterfaceName.super
            }
        }

    `A.super.hello()` is syntax you will see nowhere else in Java. It only
    exists for this situation. Plain `super.hello()` would mean the
    SUPERCLASS, which is Object here, so it would not compile.

CASE 3 -- a superclass method vs an interface default method

        class Parent { public void hello() { System.out.println("Parent"); } }
        interface Helper { default void hello() { System.out.println("Helper"); } }

        class Child extends Parent implements Helper { }

        new Child().hello();          // prints "Parent"

    No error at all. The rule is:

            THE CLASS ALWAYS WINS.

    A real method inherited from a superclass beats a default method from
    an interface, silently. Remember it as "class wins over interface", and
    be careful -- this one does not warn you.


===============================================================================
PART 8 : INTERFACE vs ABSTRACT CLASS -- THE FULL TABLE
===============================================================================

                            ABSTRACT CLASS            INTERFACE
    ----------------------  ------------------------  ----------------------
    Keyword to use it       extends                   implements
    How many per class      exactly 1                 as many as you like
    Relationship            IS-A                      CAN-DO / HAS-ABILITY
    Instance fields         YES                       NO -- never
    Constants               yes                       yes (public static final)
    Constructor             YES                       NO
    Instance blocks         yes                       no
    Static blocks           yes                       no
    Concrete methods        yes                       yes, via default (Java 8+)
    Abstract methods        yes                       yes (the default kind)
    static methods          yes, and inherited        yes, NOT inherited
    private methods         yes                       yes, with a body (Java 9+)
    final methods           yes                       no
    protected members       yes                       no -- everything public
    Access modifiers        any                       members are public
    Can extend a class      yes                       NO
    Can extend interfaces   no (it implements them)   yes, several
    Can it hold state       YES                       no
    Instantiable            no                        no
    Multiple inheritance    of state + behaviour      of type + behaviour
                            -- not allowed            -- allowed
    Speed of change         adding a method breaks    adding a default method
                            all subclasses            breaks nothing
    Typical use             shared code + shared      a capability shared by
                            data for related classes  unrelated classes
    JDK example             java.lang.Number          java.util.List
                            java.io.InputStream       java.lang.Comparable

THE THREE LINES WORTH MEMORISING

    1. An interface cannot hold STATE. That decides most arguments.
    2. You get ONE extends and UNLIMITED implements.
    3. Default to an interface; use an abstract class when you have real
       shared fields or shared code to give away.


===============================================================================
PART 9 : FUNCTIONAL, MARKER AND SEALED INTERFACES
===============================================================================

FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE -- exactly ONE abstract method

        @FunctionalInterface
        interface Calculator {
            int apply(int a, int b);        // exactly one abstract method
            default void info() { }         // defaults do NOT count
            static void help() { }          // statics do NOT count
        }

    The @FunctionalInterface annotation is optional. It does nothing at
    runtime -- it just makes the compiler check the "exactly one" rule, the
    same way @Override makes it check overriding. Use it; it documents the
    intent and catches accidents.

    WHY IT MATTERS: a functional interface can be written as a LAMBDA.

        Calculator add = (a, b) -> a + b;       // no class, no new, no name
        System.out.println(add.apply(2, 3));    // 5

    That one line replaces an entire class. This is the doorway to lambdas
    and the Stream API, both of which you will do after Collections. You do
    not need to understand the arrow syntax yet -- just know that every
    lambda in Java is secretly an implementation of a functional interface.

    Ones from the JDK you will meet immediately:

        Comparable<T>     compareTo(T o)      natural sort order
        Comparator<T>     compare(T a, T b)   a custom sort order
        Runnable          run()               a task for a thread
        Predicate<T>      test(T t)           a true/false condition

MARKER INTERFACE -- an interface with NO members at all

        interface Serializable { }          // genuinely empty

    It carries no methods; it exists purely so that code can ask
    "is this object a Serializable?" with instanceof. It is a TAG.

        Serializable, Cloneable, RandomAccess    -- all empty, all in the JDK

    Modern code usually prefers annotations for this, but the JDK is full
    of markers and they are a standard interview question.

SEALED INTERFACE (Java 17+) -- you are on 21, so this is available

        sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square { }

    Restricts WHO may implement it. Useful when you want a closed set of
    possibilities that the compiler can check exhaustively in a switch.
    Worth knowing the name now; you do not need it yet.


===============================================================================
PART 10 : WHERE YOU WILL MEET INTERFACES NEXT -- COLLECTIONS
===============================================================================

Everything in this file is about to be used on every single line of the
next topic.

        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

    List      an INTERFACE (which extends Collection, which extends Iterable)
    ArrayList a CLASS that implements it
    the line  upcasting a child object into a parent-type reference, where
              the parent type happens to be an interface

    Written this way, swapping ArrayList for LinkedList changes one line
    and breaks nothing, because every other line used only the List
    contract. That is PART 3's argument, applied.

The hierarchy you are about to learn is entirely interfaces at the top:

        Iterable          <- makes the for-each loop work
          Collection
            List          ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector
            Set           HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet
            Queue         ArrayDeque, PriorityQueue
        Map               HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap, Hashtable

And you will implement two interfaces by hand almost immediately:

        Comparable<T>     so your own class can be sorted by Collections.sort()
        Comparator<T>     to sort the same class a different way

Both are functional interfaces (PART 9). When you get there, come back and
re-read this section -- it will land differently.


===============================================================================
PART 11 : TRAPS AND INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================================

  1. Can an interface have a constructor?
        No. It has no instance state to initialise, and it is never
        instantiated.

  2. Can an interface have instance variables?
        No. Every field is public static final -- a constant.

  3. Why must the implementing method be public?
        Because the interface method is implicitly public, and an override
        may never reduce visibility.

  4. Can an interface extend a class?
        No. An interface may only extend other interfaces.

  5. Can an interface extend more than one interface?
        Yes -- unlike a class, which extends exactly one.

  6. Two interfaces have the same default method and a class implements
     both. What happens?
        Compile error. Override it, or pick one with A.super.method().
        PART 7 case 2.

  7. A superclass and an interface both provide the same method. Which
     runs?
        The class. "Class always wins", silently. PART 7 case 3.

  8. Are interface static methods inherited by implementing classes?
        NO. Call them on the interface name only. PART 6.

  9. Is an interface still 100% abstract?
        Not since Java 8. default and static methods have bodies.

 10. What is a functional interface? Why does it matter?
        Exactly one abstract method. It can be written as a lambda.

 11. What is a marker interface? Name two.
        An empty interface used as a tag: Serializable, Cloneable.

 12. When would you choose an abstract class over an interface?
        When you need shared STATE, a constructor, or non-public members.

 13. Can you create an object of an interface?
        Not directly. But you can create an anonymous class on the spot:

            Drawable d = new Drawable() {
                public void draw() { System.out.println("drawn"); }
            };

        That is NOT instantiating the interface -- the compiler generates a
        hidden class that implements it. It is the ancestor of the lambda.

 14. Can an interface be private or protected?
        A top-level interface, no -- public or default only. A NESTED
        interface, yes.

 15. What happens if an interface declares a method that Object already
     has, like toString()?
        It stays abstract but every class already inherits an
        implementation from Object, so nothing is forced. Also, such
        methods do not count towards the "exactly one" functional
        interface rule.


===============================================================================
PART 12 : PRACTICE LIST
===============================================================================

  1. Run InterfaceDemo.java in this package. Then uncomment each line
     marked ERROR, one at a time, and read what the compiler says.

  2. Do MultiFunctionMachine.java (PRACTICE 3) -- multiple interfaces on
     one class, and the diamond problem.

  3. Do HotelBookingApp.java (PRACTICE 4) -- abstract class and interface
     in the same program. This is the capstone.

  4. Reproduce PART 7 case 2 from scratch: two interfaces, same default
     method, one class. Get the error, then fix it BOTH ways.

  5. Reproduce PART 7 case 3 and confirm the class wins. Note that you get
     no warning at all.

  6. Prove PART 6's trap: put a static method in an interface and try to
     call it through the implementing class name.

  7. Rewrite src/Interface/ClassImplementsInterface.java properly: give A1
     and S2 meaningful names and a real method each, then add a default
     method to one of them.

  8. Write a @FunctionalInterface with one abstract method. Implement it
     three ways: a named class, an anonymous class, and a lambda. Compare
     the line counts. Then add a second abstract method and watch the
     annotation reject it.

  9. Take your OOPS/Polymorphism/MethodOverriding/PaymentSystem.java and
     convert Payment from a class into an interface. Note what breaks:
     WalletPayment calls super.pay(). Work out what has to change and why.
     (Hint: Payment.super.pay() -- and what that tells you about which
     tool was right for that job.)

 10. Open java.util.List in IntelliJ (Ctrl+click it). Count the abstract
     methods, the default methods and the static methods. Everything in
     this file is in that one source file.
